//  PETPORTS -- THE SETTINGS-ROW HELP TOOLTIP
//
//  THIS PATH IS THE INTERFACE, AND IT IS THE ONE THING HERE THAT CANNOT MOVE.
//  An item names a layout with `tooltipKind`, and the engine resolves that
//  string into /interface/tooltips/<kind>.tooltip. petports_helptooltip.item
//  declares "petports_help", so this file is petports_help.tooltip and a typo
//  in either is a missing asset rather than a fallback.
//
//  ---- WHAT FILLS THESE WIDGETS -----------------------------------------
//
//  NOTHING SCRIPTED. A tooltip has no script; the engine builds it from the
//  item and populates BY WIDGET NAME, touching only the names it finds. That
//  is why the vanilla layouts differ so freely -- bow.tooltip declares damage
//  labels, armor.tooltip declares a status list, and base.tooltip declares
//  neither.
//
//  SO OMISSION IS THE LEVER. Every vanilla layout carries moneyIcon,
//  priceLabel and rarityLabel; this one declares none of the three, which is
//  how a help mark stops claiming to be worth 0 pixels of currency and stops
//  announcing that it is Common. They are not hidden, they do not exist.
//
//  descriptionLabel  <- the descriptor's `description` parameter, per row
//  objectImage       <- the item's icon drawables
//  the title         <- the descriptor's `shortdescription`, stamped into the
//                       header by the engine at a fixed offset. That is why
//                       HEADER_H is 25 in petports_tooltipart.py and is not a
//                       free number.
//
//  ---- GEOMETRY ---------------------------------------------------------
//
//  150 x 131, and the art is generated: see petports_tooltipart.py, which
//  owns WIDTH, HEADER_H, BODY_H and FOOTER_H and explains why the width is
//  150 rather than the 100 this was first sketched at. Short version: the
//  tip bodies were written and measured against the pane's own 150px box at a
//  140px wrap, the longest is 163 characters, and at 100 wide the same string
//  wants ten to fourteen lines in a column half as readable.
//
//  POSITIONS ARE FROM THE BOTTOM-LEFT OF THE WHOLE TOOLTIP, not of the band
//  they sit in -- read codex.tooltip, where rarityLabel at y=23 is inside a
//  34-tall footer and objectImage at y=100 is up in the body.
//
//      y   0..  4   footer
//      y   4..106   body
//      y 106..131   header, with the title in it
//
//  THE FOUR NUMBERS THAT MOVE WITH THE ART. If WIDTH or BODY_H changes in the
//  generator, these change here in the same edit and nothing else does:
//
//      descriptionLabel.position   y is the body's top minus 6 of pad
//      descriptionLabel.wrapWidth  WIDTH - 28 - 6
//      objectImage.position        y is the text top minus half the icon
//
//  ---- THE ICON ---------------------------------------------------------
//
//  minSize AND maxSize BOTH STATED, AND EQUAL. That is the whole vanilla
//  convention -- every layout above does it -- and here it is also the fix
//  for the thing that made the default tooltip unusable: the icon was being
//  scaled to whatever the layout felt like. Inside a tooltip the layout says.
//  It does NOT fix the row's own slot, which sizes art its own way.
//
//  24, NOT 16. The source icons are 16px inventory art, so this is a clean 2x
//  minus a little. It occupies x 6..30 and the text starts at x=34, so the two
//  columns do not touch -- they overlapped by two pixels in the first draft,
//  which is the kind of thing centred positioning hides until it is drawn.

{
	"background" : {
		"type" : "background",
		"fileHeader" : "/interface/tooltips/petports_helptooltip_header.png",
		"fileBody" : "/interface/tooltips/petports_helptooltip_body.png",
		"fileFooter" : "/interface/tooltips/petports_helptooltip_footer.png"
	},

	//  CENTRED, so this is the icon's MIDDLE. y=88 puts its top edge on 100,
	//  which is the text's top edge, so the two columns start on the same line.
	"objectImage" : {
		"type" : "image",
		"position" : [ 18, 88 ],
		"file" : "",
		"centered" : true,
		"maxSize" : [ 24, 24 ],
		"minSize" : [ 24, 24 ]
	},

	//  y=100 is the body's top (106) less 6 of pad. wrapWidth is WIDTH less the
	//  x here less 6 of right pad.
	"descriptionLabel" : {
		"type" : "label",
		"position" : [ 34, 100 ],
		"hAnchor" : "left",
		"vAnchor" : "top",
		"wrapWidth" : 110
	}
}
